{
  "evidenceId": "ev-20261011-chain-lab",
  "date": "2026-10-11",
  "claim": "Modelled as the bytes actually flow (OTD -> VMulti integer counts -> RawAccel packet clock), the RawAccel engine's tracking gain noise on steep-ramp cards is large under ordinary USB jitter (Psylocke 12.0% / 0.50 deg aim error per 100 ms vs Cadence Lock 0.0127 deg). RawAccel's 'constant time interval' would fix ordinary jitter but misprices VMulti's split packets on >800 mm/s reports (~3 deg per fast flick) and any dropped report, so it stays refused for the pen. Cadence Lock was exact except for one pattern: a single report arriving LATE (a >1.5-interval gap carrying one report of travel) was priced as two merged reports at half speed (Psylocke 1.10 deg aim error at one late report per 40). The new Cadence drop check (price a gap as merged only when its travel says so, judged against the trend of the last two plain reports) removes that (1.10 -> 0.0127 deg) and is never worse than plain Cadence in a stress sweep (drops and late reports at every 3rd-17th report, 4 scenarios, 7 cards, 3 seeds; worst +0.002 deg).",
  "method": "ChainLab (AimForge --chain-lab [ids] [--late|--drop|--no-jitter], --dropcheck-stress): OpenTabletDriver v0.6.4.0 RelativeOutputMode (float px delta), VoiDPlugins VMultiRelativePointer a69fe34 (carry, split at 127, truncate, 0,0 packets), RawAccel driver.cpp 53a7213 (wall-clock packet interval clamped [1/16, 100] ms or the constant 1000/rate, zero packets only advance the clock, legacy classic/LUT gain, truncate + carry). Hand: 0.01 mm lattice at 360 Hz; micro sway 4 mm 1 Hz, tap band 12 mm 1.2 Hz, aim band 50 mm 1.5 Hz, flicks 60 mm in 110 ms min-jerk (peak ~1020 mm/s). USB: bounded arrival jitter +/-10% of an interval; late = held back to just before the next report; drop = never delivered. Scores: per-packet gain vs the gain the hand's true speed calls for (noise/bias by band) and aim error = std of the 100 ms change of delivered-minus-ideal output, degrees at the card's cm/360. Two harness bugs were found and fixed before these numbers (first-report priming of Cadence; the first packet's clock).",
